{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:53df9e8343eb1dd625e65d72384abe6943158efc0b124fd5c65693a0ded6ccbf","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.4},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: the Historian's 41 (conjunct A 0.70, conjunct B 0.65, rho -0.10, P(B|A) 0.60).\nCONJUNCT A (OECD-38 GDP-weighted core >= 2.50%/yr avg 2027-2036). Prior 0.70. The three Wave-1 seats span 0.70 / 0.42 / 0.50, and the entire spread is one identification question: does the OECD-38 GDP-weighted aggregate carry Turkey, Mexico, Colombia, Chile and Costa Rica at material weight? This is arithmetic, not judgment, so I resolve it structurally rather than by LR: Turkey alone at ~2% of OECD GDP running a 10-20% core contributes +0.3 to +0.5pp, and the OECD-wide aggregate historically ran 0.5-2.0pp above the euro-area/US core for exactly this reason (OECD aggregate CPI ~1.8-2.2% in 2015-2019 against euro-area core ~1.0%). The Fermi bottom-up build of 2.39% books \"rest 0.13 x 2.5%\", which understates the EM members; I therefore adopt the Historian's composition and REJECT the Fermi central case. EVIDENCE: US core CPI 2.6% Sep-2026 (up from a five-year low of 2.4% in Aug), euro area core 2.5% Sep-2026, US core PCE ~3.0-3.1% Aug-2026 (the Sep actual unverified). The series starts AT OR ABOVE the threshold. LR about 1.5 for A, below 2:1 so DISCOUNTED to 1.25; and most of its content is already inside the Historian's 0.70. Posterior A = 0.72.\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI avg < 1.50%/yr 2027-2036). Prior 0.65. THE MATERIAL SIGNAL, and the one that justifies this seat existing: NBS primary release of 14 Oct 2026 gives September CPI +0.9% y/y and +0.2% m/m, up from 0.8% in August and 0.5% in July, core (ex food and energy) 1.0% y/y and reported as the first 1.0% in 19 months, and PPI +5.0% y/y accelerating from +3.8% in August. Raw LR: P(this reflation signature | China 10-yr average >= 1.5%) / P(same | < 1.5%) looks like 2.2-2.5, because PPI acceleration of that size has historically led CPI by two to four quarters. I CANNOT SUSTAIN THAT LR. The 2021 episode is the counterexample inside the same regime: Chinese PPI reached roughly +13% while CPI stayed near 1%, because PPI is commodity-and-export-price driven and has been structurally decoupled from the household price level since the property bust. That precedent cuts the LR to about 1.5, below my 2:1 bar, so I DISCOUNT it to an effective 1.4 against B. Posterior: prior odds 1.857 / 1.4 = 1.326 -> 0.570; I set B = 0.62, because the threshold arithmetic is demanding in the other direction — failing B needs roughly 2.0%+ sustained from 2028 off a 2023-2026 run rate of ~0.0-0.9%, against -60M population over 2026-35, worker-to-retiree 4.4 -> 2.8, and pension subsidies already ~2.9T RMB or ~10.1% of fiscal expenditure, with the fiscal space for a reflationary household transfer being consumed by the pension transfer that will not reflate.\nCORRELATION: mildly negative, rho about -0.10, retained. Chinese deflation is exported disinflation into OECD tradables (1998-2004, 2014-2016), partially offset by a shared commodity shock. P(B|A) = 0.62 x 0.92 = 0.57.\nJOINT: 0.72 x 0.57 = 0.410 -> 41, less a one-point conjunction haircut = 40.\nDIRECTION: essentially UNMOVED, -1 point off the Historian prior of 41. This is the honest result: the two material LRs point opposite ways and cancel. The China reflation print moves B down roughly 3 points; the observed OECD core prints starting above the threshold move A up roughly 2 points. Note that my 40 sits well above the Fermi 30 and the Scout 26, and the whole gap is the OECD-38 composition identification, not a different view of inflation dynamics.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.337Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters. This directly tests the composition argument (Turkey, Mexico, Colombia, Chile, Costa Rica holding the aggregate above the G7 core) that is the load-bearing non-AI assumption in my whole payload.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 40 to about 24 (conjunct A from 0.72 to roughly 0.45). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 26 down to about 15, D from 14 up to about 24, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix the regime probabilities shift mass from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional holding to one of my best. This is the single cheapest observation that falsifies me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 (US 10y TIPS) 12-month moving average falls below 1.20% at any point, i.e. roughly 140bp below the locked 30 Oct 2026 print of 2.60%, without an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 25% (so it is a level shift, not a flight-to-quality spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 P50 falls from 2.05 to about 1.50 and P10 from 1.10 to about 0.55, restoring the Historian's Reinhart-Sbrancia repression tail that my 3:1 likelihood ratio on the TIPS anchor currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 22 to about 15 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. In the asset matrix G7 long nominals and TIPS improve across all four regimes and my 2026-2030 buildout numbers for cash and ILBs are too high.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks, and gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting (against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The 2.5:1 likelihood ratio that does most of the downward work in my CQ3 collapses. CQ3 P50 rises from 1.75 to about 1.95 and P90 from 2.65 back to about 2.90; CQ6-A rises from 11 to about 16-17 and B falls correspondingly. It also raises realized AI-Q3 datacentre load back toward the locked 998 TWh P50 and cuts my upward correction to AI-Q4. Power/grid and construction conditional returns fall (the scarcity rent disappears), AI-complex conditional returns rise.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly release) 12-month average exceeds 2.0% for four consecutive quarters, with core ex-food-and-energy above 1.8% — i.e. the PPI +5.0% signal of Sep-2026 transmits to the household price level rather than decoupling as it did in 2021.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 conjunct B (0.62) is wrong and CQ2 falls from 40 to about 22. It also means the Chinese demand deficit I have priced is resolving, which raises my China growth leg above 2.8%, lifts CQ3 P50 by roughly 0.10-0.15pp through the 0.335 PPP weight, and improves China equities across every regime while worsening onshore duration.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey or the OECD earnings database shows ISIC F+Q real median hourly wage growth leading J+K+M by >= 3.0 percentage points cumulative since 2025 (i.e. on pace for the +10pp gate), rather than the dead heat the Atlanta Fed tracker currently shows at 4.2 against 4.2.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 rises from 9 to about 25 (conjunct C2 from 0.15 to roughly 0.45), and the Stanford/ADP hiring-volume-not-pay mechanism that is my only above-2:1 signal on that question is falsified. It also means physical scarcity is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing, which raises CQ6-C from 26 toward 32 through the cost-push channel and makes construction/trades and healthcare operators materially better holdings than I have priced in regimes B and C.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'growth'."}}